# Run mcast as a service. Copy this file to /etc/mcast/NAME.conf, edit
# MCAST_ARGS, then start it with:
#
#   sudo systemctl enable --now mcast@NAME
#
# and follow its output with `journalctl -u mcast@NAME -f`.
#
# MCAST_ARGS is everything after `mcast` on the command line. systemd splits
# it on spaces and doesn't understand quotes, so pick device and stream
# names by a part without spaces (both match on any part of the name).
# A system service has no desktop sound server, so name the ALSA device
# (prefer plughw:, which converts formats the card lacks, over hw:);
# `mcast audio devices` lists them.

# Play the announced stream named like "lobby" on the Pi's headphone jack,
# with a stats line every minute:
MCAST_ARGS=audio recv --stream lobby --device plughw:CARD=Headphones --stats 60

# Other examples (keep one MCAST_ARGS line per file):
# Send a USB microphone as G.722 paging audio, voice processing on, DSCP EF:
#MCAST_ARGS=audio send --capture USB -e g722 -p -g 239.192.2.1:10000 --tos 184 --name Paging
# Loop background music:
#MCAST_ARGS=audio send --file /var/lib/mcast/music.mp3 --loop -m music --name Music
